export default {"G000033":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000033</code></p><h3>∞-topos</h3><p>An ∞-category <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is an ∞-topos if there is a small ∞-category <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal C</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and an accessible left exact localization <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒫</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒞</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal P(\\mathcal C)\\to\\mathcal X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> of its presheaves of spaces.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://www.math.ias.edu/~lurie/papers/HTT.pdf#page=547\">Lurie, Higher Topos Theory, Definition 6.1.0.4</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000033\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000051":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000051</code></p><h3>Absolute prismatic site</h3><p>Use the opposite of the category of bounded prisms (A,I) with a map Spf(A/I) → X. The topology is generated by singleton covers opposite to (p,I)-completely faithfully flat maps of prism rings.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.14735\">Bhatt–Scholze, Definition 2.3</a> · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.08229\">Bhatt–Scholze, Prisms and prismatic cohomology</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000051\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000063":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000063</code></p><h3>Adic tame site</h3><p>A variant of the étale site with the tameness of the induced extensions of residue valuations built into the morphisms. Its role and comparison with étale cohomology are developed in the cited construction.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1801.04776\">Hübner, The adic tame site</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000063\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000037":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000037</code></p><h3>Affine finite presentation Zariski site</h3><p>Finite families of principal localizations at elements generating the unit ideal form a basis for the topology. This is the restricted big affine presentation over Spec R used in the thesis, with its stated size conventions.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.03685v1\">Blechschmidt, Using the internal language of toposes in algebraic geometry, Parts II–III</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000037\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000025":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Valuative topology refinement · structure · <code>G000025</code></p><h3>Arc topology</h3><p>Use the same lifting test but restrict both valuation rings <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> to rank at most one. Rank zero includes fields. For families use the corresponding affine-local finite refinement convention.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.04725\">Bhatt–Mathew, The arc-topology</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000025\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000085":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000085</code></p><h3>Berkovich spectrum topology</h3><p>Points are bounded multiplicative seminorms with the prescribed restriction to the base field. The topology is initial for the evaluations <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>↦</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x\\mapsto |f(x)|</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> with f in A. This ordinary point-set topology is distinct from the rigid admissible site.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1090/surv/033\">Berkovich, Spectral Theory and Analytic Geometry over Non-Archimedean Fields</a> · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1010.2235\">Temkin, Introduction to Berkovich analytic spaces</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000085\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000047":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000047</code></p><h3>Big crystalline site</h3><p>An object is a divided-power thickening <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>↪</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U\\hookrightarrow T</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> over <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">ℐ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(S,\\mathcal I,\\gamma)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> with an <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>-map <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U\\to X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. A cover is a Zariski open cover of <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, with <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and its divided-power structure restricted along each open immersion.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07IB\">Stacks 07IB · big crystalline site and its coverings · Stacks 07IB · big crystalline site</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07IB\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07I6\">Stacks 07I6 · relative divided-power thickenings · Stacks 07I6 · divided power thickening</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07I6\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000047\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000097":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000097</code></p><h3>Big fppf site</h3><p>Take a size-bounded category of schemes over a base scheme X. A family covers when its maps are flat and locally of finite presentation and their images jointly cover the target.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/021M\">Stacks 021M · definition of fppf coverings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-021M\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/021L\">Stacks 021L · §34.7, fppf sites</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-021L\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000097\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000045":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000045</code></p><h3>Big fppf site of an algebraic stack</h3><p>Covering families are those whose projected family is fppf. The ambient fibred category also inherits the Zariski, étale, smooth and syntomic topologies.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06TG\">Stacks 06TG · sheaves on algebraic stacks</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/stacks-sheaves/#tag-06TG\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/06NV\">Stacks 06NV · inherited topology on a fibred category</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/stacks/#tag-06NV\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000045\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000096":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · construction · <code>G000096</code></p><h3>Bounded distributive lattice</h3><p>A lattice with least and greatest elements and distributive finite meets and joins. Its ideal completion is a coherent frame. Its prime spectrum carries the basic opens consisting of prime ideals missing a given element.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9947-1969-0251026-X\">Hochster, Prime ideal structure in commutative rings, Theorem 6</a> · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.13850\">Blechschmidt, Generalized spaces for constructive algebra</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000096\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000034":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · construction · <code>G000034</code></p><h3>Canonical Grothendieck topology</h3><p>The canonical topology <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J_{\\mathrm{can}}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> on <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal C</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> is the largest Grothendieck topology on <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal C</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> for which every representable presheaf is a sheaf.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00ZA\">Stacks 00ZA · canonical topology</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-00ZA\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0081551\">Artin–Grothendieck–Verdier, SGA 4, Tome 1, exposés II–IV</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000034\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000008":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000008</code></p><h3>Canonical topology</h3><p>The finest topology for which every representable presheaf is a sheaf. A sieve covers precisely when it is universally effective epimorphic, tested by descent of maps into every object after each base change.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00WO\">Stacks 00WO · representable sheaves and the canonical topology</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-00WO\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03NV\">Stacks 03NV · fpqc subcanonicity</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/etale-cohomology/#tag-03NV\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00ZA\">Stacks 00ZA · canonical topology</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-00ZA\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00Z9\">Stacks 00Z9 · finest topology making given presheaves sheaves</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-00Z9\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000008\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000065":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000065</code></p><h3>Cdarc topology</h3><p>Generate the topology by Zariski covers and the qcqs maps <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y\\to X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> for which every <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}(V)\\to X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> lifts to <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> for every Henselian valuation ring <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> of rank at most one. The lift is defined over the same <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2002.11647\">Elmanto–Hoyois–Iwasa–Kelly, Definition 3.1.1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000065\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000018":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000018</code></p><h3>Cdh topology</h3><p>Generated by Nisnevich and rh covers, equivalently by Nisnevich and completely decomposed proper covers.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000018\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000010":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000010</code></p><h3>Closed topology</h3><p>Finite jointly surjective families of closed immersions on the finite-type site.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000010\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000071":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · construction · <code>G000071</code></p><h3>Coherent locale</h3><p>Its frame is generated by compact elements closed under finite meets and containing the top. Equivalently the frame is the ideal completion of its bounded distributive lattice of compact elements.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.13850\">Blechschmidt, Generalized spaces for constructive algebra</a> · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129525000088\">The patch topology in univalent foundations</a> · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9947-1969-0251026-X\">Hochster, Prime ideal structure in commutative rings (1969)</a> · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2306.06745v2#page=4\">Bezhanishvili &amp; Melzer, Algebraic frames in Priestley duality, Definitions 2.1, 2.3 and 2.5(1). A coherent frame is algebraic, its compact elements are closed under binary meets, and its top is compact.</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000071\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000090":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · construction · <code>G000090</code></p><h3>Completed relative de Rham cohomology</h3><p>For smooth <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>-adic formal <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> over <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A/I</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, take derived global sections of the relative <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>-completed de Rham complex.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.4007/annals.2022.196.3.5\">Bhatt–Scholze, Prisms and prismatic cohomology, Theorem 1.8(3) and Corollary 15.4</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0FL6\">Stacks 0FL6 · de Rham cohomology</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/derham/#tag-0FL6\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000090\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000011":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000011</code></p><h3>Completely decomposed finite topology</h3><p>Finite families of finite maps with a residue-field-preserving lift of every point.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000011\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000057":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000057</code></p><h3>Complex analytic topology</h3><p>On affine finite type charts use the Euclidean topology on the complex zero set; glue along open charts. The resulting site uses ordinary open subsets of the analytification.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://www.numdam.org/item/AIF_1956__6__1_0/\">Serre, Géométrie algébrique et géométrie analytique</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000057\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000029":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000029</code></p><h3>Constructible topology</h3><p>On a spectral space take the topology generated by quasi-compact opens and their complements. The identity to the original spectral space is continuous. The topology is profinite and equals the original one exactly when the original is Hausdorff.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08YF\">Stacks 08YF · spectral spaces and the constructible topology</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-08YF\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000029\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000048":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · predicate · <code>G000048</code></p><h3>Crystal in modules</h3><p>For each morphism of thickenings with underlying map <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>:</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup><mo>→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f:T&#x27;\\to T</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, the canonical comparison <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><msub><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">ℱ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>→</mo><msub><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">ℱ</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\" lspace=\"0em\" rspace=\"0em\">′</mo></msup></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">f^*\\mathcal F_T\\to\\mathcal F_{T&#x27;}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> must be an isomorphism. Here <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">ℱ</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal F_T</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> denotes restriction to the Zariski site of <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07IS\">Stacks 07IS · definition of a crystal in modules · Stacks 07IS · crystal in modules · Stacks 07IS · common source definition</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07IS\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07IQ\">Stacks 07IQ · comparison map</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07IQ\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000048\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000088":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · construction · <code>G000088</code></p><h3>Crystalline cohomology</h3><p>Take the derived global sections of <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒪</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal O_{X/S}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> on the small crystalline site. Over the formal base in the prismatic comparison, use the relative <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>-adic crystalline cohomology of Bhatt–Scholze.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07IG\">Stacks 07IG · definition of the small crystalline site</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07IG\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07IN\">Stacks 07IN · sheaves and the crystalline structure sheaf</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07IN\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.4007/annals.2022.196.3.5\">Bhatt–Scholze, Prisms and prismatic cohomology, Theorem 5.2</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000088\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000067":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000067</code></p><h3>Derived étale site</h3><p>In the connective setting an étale map has étale π₀ and the higher homotopy groups are obtained by base change along π₀. A covering family is detected by joint surjectivity on the ordinary spectra. Use the chosen derived ring model and sheaves of spaces.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0404373\">Toën–Vezzosi, Homotopical Algebraic Geometry II, §2.2</a> · <a href=\"https://www.math.ias.edu/~lurie/papers/SAG-rootfile.pdf\">Lurie, Spectral Algebraic Geometry</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000067\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000069":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000069</code></p><h3>Derived fppf topology</h3><p>Use flat locally finitely presented morphisms, with flatness detected by π₀ flatness and base change on higher homotopy, and joint surjectivity on ordinary spectra. This uses homotopical descent, not just set-valued gluing.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0404373\">Toën–Vezzosi, Homotopical Algebraic Geometry II, §2.2</a> · <a href=\"https://www.math.ias.edu/~lurie/papers/SAG-rootfile.pdf\">Lurie, Spectral Algebraic Geometry</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000069\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000019":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000019</code></p><h3>Eh topology</h3><p>Generated by étale and rh covers, equivalently by étale and completely decomposed proper covers.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000019\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000016":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000016</code></p><h3>Envelope topology</h3><p>Finite families of proper morphisms with a residue-field-preserving lift of every point.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000016\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000091":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · construction · <code>G000091</code></p><h3>Étale cohomology of the generic fibre</h3><p>For the generic fibre <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>η</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_\\eta</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> of <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> in the sense of Bhatt–Scholze, use <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>e</mi><mo>ˊ</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>η</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝐙</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\Gamma_{\\acute{e}t}(X_\\eta,\\mathbf Z/p^n)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> with finite coefficients.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.4007/annals.2022.196.3.5\">Bhatt–Scholze, Prisms and prismatic cohomology, Theorems 1.8(4) and 9.1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000091\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000060":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000060</code></p><h3>Étale site of a locally spatial diamond</h3><p>Objects are locally separated étale maps of diamonds to X. Covers are jointly surjective families in the category of v-stacks.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.07343\">Scholze, Étale cohomology of diamonds, §§8, 14–15</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000060\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000042":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000042</code></p><h3>Étale site of an algebraic space</h3><p>Use scheme charts étale over X and jointly surjective étale covering families. Allowing algebraic spaces as objects gives an equivalent topos by the special cocontinuous comparison.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03ED\">Stacks 03ED · small étale site of an algebraic space</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/spaces-properties/#tag-03ED\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03G1\">Stacks 03G1 · comparison with algebraic spaces as objects · Stacks 03G1 · comparison of object categories</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/spaces-properties/#tag-03G1\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0H01\">Stacks 0H01 · affine étale site of an algebraic space</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/spaces-properties/#tag-0H01\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000042\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000003":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000003</code></p><h3>Étale topology</h3><p>Jointly surjective étale families.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/020K\">Stacks 020K · topologies on schemes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-020K\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0215\">Stacks 0215 · étale covering</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-0215\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000003\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000078":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000078</code></p><h3>Finite étale site</h3><p>Use jointly surjective covering families among the finite étale objects over X. Over a connected pointed scheme their fibre functor gives a Galois category, which determines the profinite étale fundamental group.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BL6\">Stacks 0BL6 · finite étale morphisms</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/pione/#tag-0BL6\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0BND\">Stacks 0BND · the étale fundamental group</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/pione/#tag-0BND\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000078\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000013":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000013</code></p><h3>Finite flat topology</h3><p>Singleton finite flat surjections. In the Noetherian setting these are precisely finite locally free surjections.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05WM\">Stacks 05WM · finite locally free refinements of fppf covers</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/more-morphisms/#tag-05WM\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000013\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000079":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000079</code></p><h3>Finite presentation h topology</h3><p>Covering sieves admit, locally on affine opens of the target, refinement by finite affine covers of a proper surjection of finite presentation. This separates the finite presentation h convention from ph, which uses proper surjections without the finite presentation hypothesis.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0ETQ\">Stacks 0ETQ · the h topology</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/flat/#tag-0ETQ\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0DBC\">Stacks 0DBC · the ph topology</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-0DBC\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000079\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000014":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000014</code></p><h3>Finite topology</h3><p>Finite jointly surjective families of finite morphisms on the finite-type site.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000014\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000044":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000044</code></p><h3>Flat-fppf site</h3><p>Objects are flat scheme maps to the stack. Covering arrows form an fppf family of the source schemes, so they are flat and locally of finite presentation.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0787\">Stacks 0787 · lisse-étale and flat-fppf definitions · Stacks 0787 · definition of the flat-fppf site · Stacks 0787 · definition of the lisse-étale and flat-fppf sites</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/stacks-cohomology/#tag-0787\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0H0Y\">Stacks 0H0Y · cohomological comparison</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/stacks-perfect/#tag-0H0Y\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GR0\">Stacks 0GR0 · cohomological comparison</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/stacks-cohomology/#tag-0GR0\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000044\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000098":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000098</code></p><h3>Formal small étale site</h3><p>For a p-adic formal scheme X, use étale formal schemes over X and jointly surjective étale covers. Reduction identifies the site with the small étale site of the special fibre.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://people.mpim-bonn.mpg.de/scholze/prisms.pdf#page=37\">Bhatt–Scholze, Prisms and prismatic cohomology, Construction 4.4</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0DE9\">Stacks 0DE9 · §87.34, the small étale site of a formal algebraic space</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/formal-spaces/#tag-0DE9\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000098\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000006":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000006</code></p><h3>Fppf topology</h3><p>Jointly surjective flat morphisms locally of finite presentation. Over a locally Noetherian target, finite flat maps are finite locally free; in general use finite locally free maps in the generation theorem.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05WM\">Stacks 05WM · finite locally free refinements of fppf covers</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/more-morphisms/#tag-05WM\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/020K\">Stacks 020K · topologies on schemes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-020K\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/021M\">Stacks 021M · fppf covering</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-021M\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0DET\">Stacks 0DET · étale-local finite locally free refinement</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/more-morphisms/#tag-0DET\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000006\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000007":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000007</code></p><h3>Fpqc topology</h3><p>Flat families for which each affine open of the target is covered by images of finitely many quasi-compact opens in the sources that map into that affine open. In particular a faithfully flat quasi-compact morphism is a singleton cover.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/022A\">Stacks 022A · fpqc coverings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-022A\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03NV\">Stacks 03NV · the fpqc topology</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/etale-cohomology/#tag-03NV\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/022B\">Stacks 022B · fpqc covering</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-022B\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000007\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000022":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Valuative topology refinement · structure · <code>G000022</code></p><h3>Fpqc topology</h3><p>Use the fpqc families from the classical comparison, restricted to this category.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0ETA\">Stacks 0ETA · valuation lifting and comparison with fpqc and ph</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-0ETA\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000022\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000056":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000056</code></p><h3>Full log étale site</h3><p>Uses log étale morphisms in Nakayama’s full logarithmic site, which incorporates log modifications and is distinct from the Kummer étale site. The cover definition and use of the fs fibre product are as in the cited paper.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001870815303935\">Nakayama, Logarithmic étale cohomology, II</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000056\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000039":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000039</code></p><h3>Gros étale site</h3><p>Objects are arbitrary X-schemes in the chosen category; covers are jointly surjective étale families. The covering condition is shared with the small étale site, but the objects differ.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/021B\">Stacks 021B · big and small étale sites</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-021B\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0081551\">Artin–Grothendieck–Verdier, SGA 4, Tome 1, exposés II–IV</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0215\">Stacks 0215 · étale covering</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-0215\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/040S\">Stacks 040S · geometric points on the big site</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/etale-cohomology/#tag-040S\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000039\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000036":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000036</code></p><h3>Gros Zariski site</h3><p>Arrows are S-morphisms. A covering family consists of open immersions with images covering the target. This is a different presentation and category from the open subsets of S.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/020T\">Stacks 020T · big and small Zariski sites</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-020T\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.03685v1\">Blechschmidt, Using the internal language of toposes in algebraic geometry, Parts II–III</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000036\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000030":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000030</code></p><h3>Grothendieck site</h3><p>A category equipped with a Grothendieck topology. A basis of covering families in the sense of Stacks 00VH induces a topology of covering sieves.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0081551\">Artin–Grothendieck–Verdier, SGA 4, Tome 1, exposés II–IV</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00VH\">Stacks 00VH · sites given by covering families</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-00VH\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00Z4\">Stacks 00Z4 · Grothendieck topologies · Stacks 00Z4 · topology in terms of covering sieves</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-00Z4\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00ZD\">Stacks 00ZD · associated topology</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-00ZD\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/site\">nLab, site (bibliographic index)</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00ZC\">Stacks 00ZC · passage from a site to a topology</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-00ZC\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000030\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000031":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000031</code></p><h3>Grothendieck topos</h3><p>A category equivalent to the category of sheaves of sets on a small site. Different sites may present equivalent topoi.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0081551\">Artin–Grothendieck–Verdier, SGA 4, Tome 1, exposés II–IV</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00XA\">Stacks 00XA · topos and morphism of topoi · Stacks 00XA · topos and morphisms of topoi · Stacks 00XA · common source definition</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-00XA\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00Y4\">Stacks 00Y4 · point of a topos</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites/#tag-00Y4\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000031\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000021":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000021</code></p><h3>H topology</h3><p>Generated by Zariski open covers and proper surjections on the finite-type site. Equivalently, use finite families whose coproduct is a universal topological epimorphism.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000021\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000072":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000072</code></p><h3>Hochster dual topology</h3><p>Use complements of quasi-compact open subsets of X as a basis. Duality reverses specialization, is involutive on spectral spaces and does not change the patch topology.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9947-1969-0251026-X\">Hochster, Prime ideal structure in commutative rings (1969)</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08YF\">Stacks 08YF · spectral spaces and the constructible topology</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-08YF\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000072\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000089":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · construction · <code>G000089</code></p><h3>Hodge–Tate differential forms</h3><p>For <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spf</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=\\operatorname{Spf}(R)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> smooth over a bounded prism quotient <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A/I</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, take the <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>-completed modules of differentials <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">{</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\widehat{\\Omega}^i_{R/(A/I)}\\{-i\\}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. The twist uses the invertible <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>I</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A/I</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>-module <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msup><mi>I</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I/I^2</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.4007/annals.2022.196.3.5\">Bhatt–Scholze, Prisms and prismatic cohomology, Theorems 1.8(2) and 6.3</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000089\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000084":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000084</code></p><h3>Huber adic spectrum topology</h3><p>Points are continuous valuations up to equivalence that are at most 1 on A⁺. Rational subsets, specified by finitely many inequalities <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mi>g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∣</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">|f_i|\\le |g|\\ne0</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> with the open-ideal condition, form a basis of quasi-compact opens. The adic spectrum is spectral.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02571668\">Huber, Continuous valuations, §3</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000084\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000053":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000053</code></p><h3>Infinitesimal site</h3><p>In the small formulation use open U of X and nilpotent thickenings U ↪ T over S with coverings induced by opens of T. There are no divided powers in this definition.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://www.numdam.org/item/PMIHES_1975__45__5_0/\">Hartshorne, On the de Rham cohomology of algebraic varieties</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07IB\">Stacks 07IB · big crystalline site and its coverings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07IB\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000053\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000054":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000054</code></p><h3>Kummer étale site</h3><p>Kummer log étale maps give coverings when their underlying maps are jointly surjective. The Kummer condition is imposed on characteristic monoids at geometric points.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://smf.emath.fr/publications/travaux-de-k-fujiwara-k-kato-et-c-nakayama-sur-la-cohomologie-etale-logarithmique\">Illusie, Kummer étale cohomology and log blow-up invariance</a> · <a href=\"https://www.imo.universite-paris-saclay.fr/~luc.illusie/lfd.pdf\">Illusie–Nakayama–Tsuji, On log flat descent</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000054\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000055":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000055</code></p><h3>Kummer flat site</h3><p>Use jointly surjective Kummer log flat covering families with the finite presentation condition in Kato’s convention. Pullbacks are in the fs category, which may require saturation.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.10678\">Kato, Logarithmic structures of Fontaine–Illusie II</a> · <a href=\"https://www.imo.universite-paris-saclay.fr/~luc.illusie/lfd.pdf\">Illusie–Nakayama–Tsuji, On log flat descent</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000055\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000020":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000020</code></p><h3>ℓdh topology</h3><p>Fix a prime ℓ. Adjoin prime-to-ℓ finite flat surjections to cdh covers.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000020\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000043":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000043</code></p><h3>Lisse-étale site</h3><p>Objects are smooth maps from schemes, with the usual triangles up to a specified 2-isomorphism. Covering families project to jointly surjective étale families of schemes.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0787\">Stacks 0787 · lisse-étale and flat-fppf definitions · Stacks 0787 · definition of the lisse-étale and flat-fppf sites</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/stacks-cohomology/#tag-0787\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07BG\">Stacks 07BG · failure of functoriality for arbitrary morphisms</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/examples/#tag-07BG\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GR2\">Stacks 0GR2 · functoriality for smooth maps</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/stacks-cohomology/#tag-0GR2\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07AT\">Stacks 07AT · functoriality</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/stacks-cohomology/#tag-07AT\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000043\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000070":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · construction · <code>G000070</code></p><h3>Locale</h3><p>The category of locales is the opposite of the category of frames. A map of locales is specified contravariantly by the inverse-image homomorphism of their frames of opens.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.13850\">Blechschmidt, Generalized spaces for constructive algebra</a> · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1017/S0960129525000088\">The patch topology in univalent foundations</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000070\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000099":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000099</code></p><h3>Localic topos</h3><p>The topos of sheaves on a locale. In the frame presentation the objects are opens, arrows are inclusions, and a family covers an open when its join is that open.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2012.13850\">Blechschmidt, Generalized spaces for constructive algebra</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/009H\">Stacks 009H · sheaves on a basis</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sheaves/#tag-009H\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000099\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000092":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000092</code></p><h3>Locally finitely presented big Zariski site</h3><p>Objects are schemes locally of finite presentation over the chosen base scheme. Covers are jointly surjective families of open immersions.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.03685\">Blechschmidt, Using the internal language of toposes, §15</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/020S\">Stacks 020S · big Zariski site</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-020S\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000092\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000066":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · construction · <code>G000066</code></p><h3>Motivic Nisnevich presentation</h3><p>Start from Nisnevich sheaves of spaces and impose A¹-invariance by localization at the projections X × A¹ → X. This localization is additional to the sheaf condition.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0805.4576\">Voevodsky, Unstable motivic homotopy categories in Nisnevich and cdh-topologies</a> · <a href=\"https://www.numdam.org/item/PMIHES_1999__90__45_0/\">Morel–Voevodsky, A¹-homotopy theory of schemes</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000066\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000002":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000002</code></p><h3>Nisnevich topology</h3><p>Completely decomposed étale families: every target point has a lift whose induced residue-field map is an isomorphism.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000002\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000083":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000083</code></p><h3>p-complete arc site</h3><p>A singleton cover passes the arc lifting test against p-complete valuation rings of rank at most one, with extensions also p-complete and of rank at most one. Mathew distinguishes this from the variant testing rank one and requiring p to be nonzero.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1112/S0010437X21007144\">Mathew, On K(1)-local TR, Definitions 1.6 and 7.1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000083\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000062":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000062</code></p><h3>Perfectoid v-site</h3><p>A family covers if each quasi-compact open of the target can be covered by images of finitely many quasi-compact opens in its sources. The site is used for diamonds and v-sheaves; it does not share the underlying category of the scheme v-site.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.07343\">Scholze, Étale cohomology of diamonds, §§8, 14–15</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000062\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000038":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000038</code></p><h3>Petit étale site</h3><p>Objects are étale maps to X and covers are jointly surjective étale families. A geometric point gives the stalk defined by the filtered system of pointed étale neighbourhoods.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/021B\">Stacks 021B · big and small étale sites</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-021B\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/04HP\">Stacks 04HP · the étale topos</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/etale-cohomology/#tag-04HP\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0215\">Stacks 0215 · étale covering</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-0215\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03PU\">Stacks 03PU · stalkwise exactness</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/etale-cohomology/#tag-03PU\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000038\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000077":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000077</code></p><h3>Petit fppf site</h3><p>Use the small flat site convention: objects are flat, locally finitely presented X-schemes in a chosen universe, and arrows are X-morphisms. A family covers when its members are flat and locally of finite presentation and their images jointly cover its target.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://www.numdam.org/item/10.5802/jtnb.863.pdf\">Mézard–Romagny–Tossici, Sekiguchi–Suwa theory revisited, Appendix A (small flat site)</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/021M\">Stacks 021M · fppf covering families</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-021M\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000077\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000040":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000040</code></p><h3>Petit Nisnevich site</h3><p>An étale family covers if every target point lifts to a point inducing an isomorphism of residue fields. Joint surjectivity alone does not give this condition.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/0805.4576\">Voevodsky, Unstable motivic homotopy categories in Nisnevich and cdh-topologies</a> · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/978-94-009-2399-7_11\">Nisnevich, The completely decomposed topology on schemes</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000040\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000035":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000035</code></p><h3>Petit Zariski site</h3><p>Objects are opens, arrows are inclusions and coverings are ordinary open covers. Its sheaves are the usual sheaves on the underlying Zariski space.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/020T\">Stacks 020T · big and small Zariski sites</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-020T\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.03685v1\">Blechschmidt, Using the internal language of toposes in algebraic geometry, Parts II–III</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/020X\">Stacks 020X · small Zariski site and usual topology</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-020X\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000035\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000023":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Valuative topology refinement · structure · <code>G000023</code></p><h3>Ph topology</h3><p>Use Zariski covers and proper surjections as generators. The Stacks Project distinguishes ph from its finite-presentation h convention. Over the separated finite-type Noetherian site of the classical comparison these are the same generators used there for h.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0DBC\">Stacks 0DBC · the ph topology</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-0DBC\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EVM\">Stacks 0EVM · affine inverse limits of h and ph covers</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/proetale/#tag-0EVM\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000023\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000012":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000012</code></p><h3>Prime-to-ℓ finite flat topology</h3><p>Fix a prime ℓ. Use singleton finite flat surjections of constant rank d with ℓ ∤ d. Such maps are finite locally free in the finite-type Noetherian setting.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000012\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000049":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · construction · <code>G000049</code></p><h3>Prism</h3><p>A prism is a δ-ring A with an ideal I defining an effective Cartier divisor, such that A is derived (p,I)-complete and <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>I</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\in I+\\varphi(I)A</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.08229\">Bhatt–Scholze, Prisms and prismatic cohomology</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000049\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000082":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000082</code></p><h3>Pro-cdh site on formal schemes</h3><p>Use Nisnevich covers and formal abstract blowup covers. This presentation uses the formal completion at a center in the formal scheme category; the pro-cdh site on ordinary schemes uses the family of its finite thickenings.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.14295\">Kelly–Saito, A pro-cdh topology on formal schemes</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000082\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000081":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000081</code></p><h3>Pro-cdh site on schemes</h3><p>Generate the topology by Zariski coverings, distinguished Nisnevich coverings and pro-abstract blowup coverings. For a proper map in the category that is an isomorphism off a finitely presented closed subscheme, the last covering family contains the proper map and every positive-order thickening of the center.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.02699\">Kelly–Saito, A procdh topology, Definitions 1.1 and 2.1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000081\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000080":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000080</code></p><h3>Pro-Nisnevich site</h3><p>Covering families are pro-étale and fpqc. Their disjoint union is completely decomposed, meaning that every target point lifts with unchanged residue field.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.17314\">Mattis, The pro-Nisnevich topology, Definitions 2.1–2.2</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000080\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000028":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000028</code></p><h3>Profinite site for condensed sets</h3><p>On a size-bounded category of profinite spaces, covers are finite families of jointly surjective continuous maps. With the strong limit size convention of the cited lectures, sheaves of sets are the corresponding condensed sets.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2605.03658\">Scholze, Lectures on Condensed Mathematics, §2</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000028\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000015":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000015</code></p><h3>Proper topology</h3><p>Finite jointly surjective families of proper morphisms on the finite-type site.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01W0\">Stacks 01W0 · proper morphisms</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/morphisms/#tag-01W0\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000015\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000027":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000027</code></p><h3>Qc topology on locally quasi-compact spaces</h3><p>Objects are locally quasi-compact Hausdorff spaces and maps are continuous. A family covers if every target point has a neighbourhood that is the union of images of finitely many quasi-compact subsets of the sources, in the sense that the union contains an open neighbourhood of the point.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/09WY\">Stacks 09WY · the qc topology and comparison with open covers</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites-cohomology/#tag-09WY\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000027\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000026":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000026</code></p><h3>Qc Zariski basis site</h3><p>For a qcqs scheme X, take its quasi-compact open subsets, with inclusions as arrows and finite open covers as covering families. Intersections stay in the basis, and restriction identifies Zariski sheaves with sheaves on this site.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0069\">Stacks 0069 · quasi-compact opens and finite refinements</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-0069\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01KH\">Stacks 01KH · quasi-separated schemes and morphisms</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/schemes/#tag-01KH\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/009H\">Stacks 009H · sheaves on a basis</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sheaves/#tag-009H\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000026\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000009":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000009</code></p><h3>Qfh topology</h3><p>On the finite-type site one may take finite families of quasi-finite maps whose coproduct is a universal topological epimorphism (a quotient map after all base changes).</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000009\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000061":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000061</code></p><h3>Quasi-pro-étale site of a diamond</h3><p>Objects are locally separated quasi-pro-étale maps of diamonds to X. Quasi-pro-étaleness is tested after pulling back to strictly totally disconnected perfectoid spaces. Covers are jointly surjective families in the category of v-stacks.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1709.07343\">Scholze, Étale cohomology of diamonds, §§8, 14–15</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000061\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000052":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000052</code></p><h3>Quasisyntomic site</h3><p>An object is a <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>-complete ring <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> with bounded <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>-power torsion for which <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">𝐙</mi><mi>p</mi></msub></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_{A/\\mathbf Z_p}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> has <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>-complete Tor-amplitude in <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[-1,0]</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. Singleton covers are opposite to <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>-completely faithfully flat maps <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A\\to B</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> for which <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>L</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L_{B/A}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> has <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>-complete Tor-amplitude in <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">[-1,0]</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1802.03261\">Bhatt–Morrow–Scholze, Topological Hochschild homology and integral p-adic Hodge theory, Definitions 1.7 and 4.10</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000052\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000087":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · construction · <code>G000087</code></p><h3>Relative prismatic cohomology</h3><p><span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>R</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Γ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mi>A</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></msub><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><msub><mi class=\"math-euler-script\" mathvariant=\"normal\">𝒪</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R\\Gamma_\\Delta(X/A)=R\\Gamma((X/A)_\\Delta,\\mathcal O_\\Delta)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, where the structure sheaf evaluates to the prism ring. The complex has a semilinear Frobenius.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.4007/annals.2022.196.3.5\">Bhatt–Scholze, Prisms and prismatic cohomology, Definition 4.1 and Theorem 1.8</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000087\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000050":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000050</code></p><h3>Relative prismatic site</h3><p>Use bounded prisms (B,IB) over (A,I) equipped with a map Spf(B/IB) → X over A/I. The topology is generated by singleton covers opposite to completely faithfully flat maps of prism rings for the ideal (p,IB).</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.08229\">Bhatt–Scholze, Definition 3.2, Corollary 3.12 and Definition 4.1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000050\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000064":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000064</code></p><h3>Relative tame site of schemes</h3><p>Use étale objects and require, in addition to covering points, tame lifts of the valuations in the relative sense. The definition depends on the chosen base, and the test is stronger than joint surjectivity.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.05468\">Hübner–Schmidt, The tame site of a scheme</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000064\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000017":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000017</code></p><h3>Rh topology</h3><p>Generated by Zariski coverings and completely decomposed proper coverings.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000017\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000058":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000058</code></p><h3>Rigid analytic admissible G-topology</h3><p>The site uses admissible open subspaces and admissible coverings. On affinoids the admissible cover condition is tested using finite affinoid refinements; arbitrary unions of point sets do not supply the cover condition.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://math.stanford.edu/~conrad/papers/aws.pdf\">Conrad, Several approaches to non-archimedean geometry</a> · <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/G-topology\">nLab, G-topology (bibliographic index)</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000058\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000059":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000059</code></p><h3>Rigid analytic pro-étale site</h3><p>The site uses pro-étale objects obtained locally from inverse systems of étale objects with eventual finite étale transition maps, using the coverings of Scholze §3. This is distinct from the weakly étale scheme site.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.3463\">Scholze, p-adic Hodge theory for rigid-analytic varieties</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000059\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000032":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000032</code></p><h3>Ringed topos</h3><p>A topos with a sheaf of rings as structure sheaf. Sheaves of modules, and their derived global sections, depend on this extra structure.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01D3\">Stacks 01D3 · definition of ringed topoi · Stacks 01D3 · ringed topos · Stacks 01D3 · common source definition</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/sites-modules/#tag-01D3\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0081551\">Artin–Grothendieck–Verdier, SGA 4, Tome 1, exposés II–IV</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000032\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000041":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000041</code></p><h3>Scheme pro-étale site</h3><p>Objects are weakly étale over X, so their structure maps and diagonals are flat. The covering families satisfy the fpqc covering condition, including the finite quasi-compact refinement condition.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1309.1198\">Bhatt–Scholze, The pro-étale topology for schemes, §4</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/098K\">Stacks 098K · the small pro-étale site · Stacks 098K · big and small pro-étale sites</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/proetale/#tag-098K\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/03NW\">Stacks 03NW · fpqc coverings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/etale-cohomology/#tag-03NW\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000041\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000046":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000046</code></p><h3>Small crystalline site</h3><p>In the big crystalline site take the full subcategory on thickenings <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi>U</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>T</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>δ</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(U,T,\\delta)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> with <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">U\\to X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> an open immersion. Covers arise by restricting a thickening to an open cover of <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07IG\">Stacks 07IG · small crystalline site</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07IG\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07IB\">Stacks 07IB · big crystalline site and its coverings</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07IB\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07MF\">Stacks 07MF · base hypotheses</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07MF\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/07I6\">Stacks 07I6 · divided power thickening</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/crystalline/#tag-07I6\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000046\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000004":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000004</code></p><h3>Smooth topology</h3><p>Jointly surjective smooth families. Smooth surjections admit sections étale locally on the target, so their generated topology is already étale.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/055U\">Stacks 055U · étale-local sections of smooth morphisms</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/more-morphisms/#tag-055U\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/020K\">Stacks 020K · topologies on schemes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-020K\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/021Z\">Stacks 021Z · smooth covering</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-021Z\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000004\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000068":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000068</code></p><h3>Spectral étale site</h3><p>Étaleness is characterized by étale π₀ and base change for the homotopy groups; covers are detected on ordinary spectra of π₀. Spectral here refers to ring spectra, a different usage from Hochster’s spectral spaces.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://www.math.ias.edu/~lurie/papers/SAG-rootfile.pdf\">Lurie, Spectral Algebraic Geometry</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000068\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000073":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · construction · <code>G000073</code></p><h3>Spectral map</h3><p>Inverse images of quasi-compact opens are quasi-compact. The corresponding pullback on bounded distributive lattices preserves finite joins and meets.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9947-1969-0251026-X\">Hochster, Prime ideal structure in commutative rings (1969)</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08YG\">Stacks 08YG · spectral spaces and spectral maps · Stacks 08YG · common source definition</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topology/#tag-08YG\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://api.pageplace.de/preview/DT0400.9781108609586_A36703407/preview-9781108609586_A36703407.pdf#page=35\">Dickmann, Schwartz &amp; Tressl, Spectral Spaces, §1.3, immediately before Proposition 1.3.6. Between spectral spaces, inverse images of quasi-compact opens must be quasi-compact open.</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000073\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000086":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Sites and presentations · structure · <code>G000086</code></p><h3>Spectral Zariski site</h3><p>Basic opens of an affine spectral scheme come from inverting elements of π₀. A family of such localizations covers when the corresponding basic opens cover the ordinary spectrum of π₀.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://www.math.ias.edu/~lurie/papers/SAG-rootfile.pdf\">Lurie, Spectral Algebraic Geometry</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000086\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000005":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000005</code></p><h3>Syntomic topology</h3><p>Jointly surjective syntomic families. Syntomic means flat, locally of finite presentation, with local complete intersection geometric fibres.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/020K\">Stacks 020K · topologies on schemes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-020K\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/01UB\">Stacks 01UB · syntomic morphisms</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/morphisms/#tag-01UB\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0225\">Stacks 0225 · syntomic covering</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-0225\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000005\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000024":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Valuative topology refinement · structure · <code>G000024</code></p><h3>Valuative (v) topology</h3><p>For a map of qcqs schemes <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y\\to X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>, test every <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}V\\to X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> for a valuation ring <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. There must be an injective local map of valuation rings <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>↪</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V\\hookrightarrow W</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> and a lift <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Spec</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Spec}W\\to Y</annotation></semantics></math></span></span> over <span class=\"atlas-math\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>. For families, work affine locally on the target and take finite affine refinements whose disjoint union passes the test.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1807.04725\">Bhatt–Mathew, The arc-topology</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0ETA\">Stacks 0ETA · valuation lifting and comparison with fpqc and ph</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-0ETA\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0EVM\">Stacks 0EVM · affine inverse limits of h and ph covers</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/proetale/#tag-0EVM\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000024\">Open shared record</a></p></article>","G000001":"<article class=\"algebra-definition atlas-panel\"><p class=\"property-context\">Classical topology refinement · structure · <code>G000001</code></p><h3>Zariski topology</h3><p>Jointly surjective open immersions. On the finite-type site one can take finite families, including the empty cover of the empty scheme.</p><p class=\"property-references\"><a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/020K\">Stacks 020K · topologies on schemes</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-020K\">Index</a> · <a href=\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1407.5782\">Gabber–Kelly, Definition 2.5, Theorem 2.6 and Table 1</a> · <a href=\"https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/020O\">Stacks 020O · Zariski covering</a> <a class=\"algebra-index-ref\" href=\"../database/stacks/topologies/#tag-020O\">Index</a></p><p class=\"algebra-links\"><a href=\"../database/#G000001\">Open shared record</a></p></article>"};
